# Copy to apps/mobile/.env and fill in. That file is gitignored — never commit
# real values, and never hardcode them in source.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE IS READ FROM
#   Expo loads .env from the PROJECT ROOT, which is the folder holding app.json
#   — apps/mobile, not the repo root. Metro must therefore be started from
#   apps/mobile, or these variables are simply absent and the app boots into
#   the "App is not configured" screen.
#
#       cd apps/mobile && npx expo start --clear
#
#   Expo prints "env: load .env" / "env: export EXPO_PUBLIC_..." on startup,
#   and the app logs "[muthoy] Supabase config loaded — host=...". If you do
#   not see those lines, the wrong directory is being used.
#
# HOW THE VALUES REACH THE APP
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_* variables are inlined by babel-preset-expo at TRANSFORM time,
#   not read at runtime. Two consequences:
#     1. Changing a value needs a Metro restart with --clear; the old value is
#        otherwise served straight from the transform cache.
#     2. Only literal `process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_FOO` member reads are inlined.
#        Destructuring process.env or computing the key yields undefined.
#
# CLOUD (EAS) BUILDS
#   EAS build machines never see this file — it is gitignored. Each build
#   profile in eas.json names an EAS environment, so create the variables once
#   per environment instead:
#
#       eas env:create --environment development --name EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
#       eas env:create --environment development --name EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#
#   Repeat for preview and production. The anon key is a public, RLS-protected
#   client key — but the service-role key must NEVER appear here, in .env, or
#   anywhere else that reaches the app bundle.

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
